            {
              "id": "glog-b1c0098a-4e84-46e9-add2-4e3b883186c0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability associated with the use of `strlen(buf.data())` in C++ arises from the potential for reading beyond the bounds of a buffer. The `strlen` function calculates the length of a C-style string by searching for the null-terminator (`'\\0'`). If the buffer is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null byte, which can lead to undefined behavior, including crashes or information disclosure.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Ensure Null-Termination**: Always ensure that buffers are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer alternatives like `std::string` or `std::vector<char>` which manage their own size and provide bounds-checked access.\n3. **Bounds Checking**: Implement bounds checking to ensure that operations do not exceed the buffer's allocated size.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `strlen(buf.data())`, use `std::string` which inherently manages the string length:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n\nint main() {\n    std::string buf = \"example\";\n    // Safe way to get the length of the string\n    size_t length = buf.length();\n    std::cout << \"Length of buffer: \" << length << std::endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the above code example, the following standard library headers are required:\n\n- `<iostream>`: For input and output stream operations.\n- `<string>`: For using the `std::string` class.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration\n\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
